The third generation GLA, revealed on 29 July 2026, unifies both internal combustion engine and battery electric (EQA) models under the GLA name, similar to the [[Mercedes-Benz CLA#C174|third-generation CLA]] revealed a year prior.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Alaniz |first=Anthony |date=30 July 2026 |title=Mercedes Reinvents Its Smallest SUV For The EV Era |url=https://www.motor1.com/news/803141/2028-mercedes-benz-gla-powertrains-specs-trims/ |access-date=30 July 2026 |website=Motor1}}</ref> While battery electric versions will be available first, the hybrid version will follow in 2027.

Compared to its predecessor, the third generation GLA is {{Convert|143|mm|in|abbr=on}} longer and {{Convert|20|mm|in|abbr=on}} lower than its predecessor with a {{Convert|2.4|in|mm|abbr=on}} longer wheelbase.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |title=New Mercedes GLA revealed as Audi Q4 rival with 408-mile range {{!}} Autocar |url=https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/electric-cars/new-mercedes-gla-revealed-audi-q4-rival-408-mile-range |access-date=2026-07-29 |website=www.autocar.co.uk |language=en}}</ref>

The exterior and interior design of the third generation GLA has been completely redesigned. The battery electric version features Mercedes-Benz's "Iconic Grille", similar to that of the [[Mercedes-Benz GLC Electric|GLC electric]], while the hybrid version uses a star pattern instead. The interior uses the MBOS Superscreen featured in various other models, consisting of a 14-inch central display, 14-inch passenger display and 10.25-inch driver display.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Padeanu |first=Adrian |date=2026-07-29 |title=Mercedes Fires Back At BMW X1 And iX1 With The New GLA |url=https://www.bmwblog.com/2026/07/29/2027-mercedes-gla-revealed-bmw-x1-competitor/ |access-date=2026-07-29 |website=BMWBLOG |language=en-US}}</ref> It has a {{Convert|410|L|cuft|abbr=on}} trunk that expands to {{Convert|1400|L|cuft|abbr=on}} when the second row is folded down; battery electric models add a {{Convert|107|L|cuft|abbr=on}} frunk.

Three battery electric versions are available - the GLA 200 electric with {{Convert|221|hp|kW|abbr=on}}, GLA 250+ electric with {{Convert|268|hp|kW|abbr=on}} and the GLA 350 4Matic electric with {{Convert|349|hp|kW|abbr=on}} and a 0-100 km/h acceleration time of 5.3 seconds. All versions use a [[Lithium nickel manganese cobalt oxides|nickel-manganese-cobalt (NMC) battery]], featuring a usable capacity of either 58.0 kWh (GLA 200 electric) or 85.0 kWh. Mercedes-Benz claims the GLA electric will offer up to {{Convert|657|km|mi|abbr=on}} of range and can charge up to 320 kW.<ref name=":2" />

